Solid principles
• S – Single Responsibility Principle (SRP): A class should have only one responsibility and therefore only one reason to change.
• O – Open/Closed Principle (OCP): Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.
• L – Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP): A subclass should be able to replace its parent class without affecting program correctness.
• I – Interface Segregation Principle (ISP): Clients should not be forced to implement or depend on methods they don't use.
• D – Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP): High-level modules should depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations.
Java 8 Quick Revision Notes (One-Liners)
Java 8 Features
• Java 8 Features: Lambda, Functional Interface, Stream API, Method Reference, Optional, Default/Static Methods, New Date-Time API, Nashorn.
Lambda Expression
• Lambda Expression: Anonymous function used to implement a functional interface.
• Syntax: (parameters) -> expression or (parameters) -> { statements; }
• Internal Working: Uses invokedynamic to create functional interface instances at runtime.
Functional Interface
• Functional Interface: Interface with exactly one abstract method (SAM).
• Purpose: Supports Lambda Expressions and Method References.
• Examples: Runnable, Callable, Comparable, Predicate, Consumer, Supplier.
• @FunctionalInterface: Ensures only one abstract method exists.
Runnable vs Callable
• Runnable: No return value, cannot throw checked exceptions.
• Callable: Returns value and can throw checked exceptions.
Default Methods
• Default Method: Interface method with implementation using default keyword.
• Why Introduced: Backward compatibility and interface evolution.
Multiple Inheritance in Java 8
• Achieved Through: Multiple interfaces with default methods.
Predefined Functional Interfaces
• Predicate: Takes input, returns boolean.
• Function<T,R>: Takes input, returns result.
• Consumer: Takes input, returns nothing.
• Supplier: No input, returns value.
• BiFunction<T,U,R>: Takes two inputs, returns result.
Method Reference
• Method Reference: Short form of lambda for calling existing methods.
• Types: Static Method, Instance Method, Constructor Reference.
Streams API
Stream API
• Stream API: Functional way to process collections of data.
Stream vs Collection
• Collection: Stores data.
• Stream: Processes data.
Intermediate Operations
• Intermediate Operations: Return another stream and are lazy.
• Examples: filter(), map(), sorted(), distinct(), limit(), skip().
Terminal Operations
• Terminal Operations: Produce result and trigger execution.
• Examples: collect(), forEach(), count(), reduce(), findFirst().
Without Terminal Operation
• No Terminal Operation: Stream pipeline never executes.
Why Streams Are Faster
• Reason: Lazy evaluation, internal iteration, parallel processing.
map() vs flatMap()
• map(): One-to-one transformation.
• flatMap(): One-to-many transformation and flattening.
Collectors
• Collectors: Used to collect stream results into List, Set, Map, String, etc.
• Used With: collect() terminal operation.
Optional Class
Optional
• Optional: Container object used to avoid NullPointerException.
Optional Methods
• Methods: of(), ofNullable(), empty(), get(), isPresent(), orElse(), map(), flatMap(), filter().
of() vs ofNullable() vs empty()
• of(): Accepts non-null value only.
• ofNullable(): Accepts null and non-null.
• empty(): Creates empty Optional.
Date & Time API
Java 8 Date-Time API
• Purpose: Thread-safe replacement for Date and Calendar.
• Classes: LocalDate, LocalTime, LocalDateTime, ZonedDateTime, Instant.
Interface Improvements
Static Method in Interface
• Static Method: Belongs to interface and called using interface name.
Default vs Static Method
• Default: Inherited and can be overridden.
• Static: Utility method, cannot be overridden.
HashMap Java 8
HashMap
• HashMap: Stores data as key-value pairs.
Internal Working
• Data Structure: Array of buckets.
• Index Formula: (n - 1) & hash.
• Collision Handling: Linked List → Red-Black Tree.
• Default Load Factor: 0.75.
Collision Handling Java 8
• Bucket Size > 8: Convert Linked List to Red-Black Tree.
• Bucket Size < 6: Convert Tree back to Linked List.
• Capacity ≥ 64: Treeification allowed.
Time Complexity
• Average: O(1)
• Worst Case (Tree): O(log n)
Nashorn Engine
• Nashorn: JavaScript engine introduced in Java 8 to execute JavaScript within JVM.
Memory Management
PermGen vs MetaSpace
• PermGen: Fixed-size JVM memory area.
• MetaSpace: Uses native memory and grows dynamically.
