Kafka Quick Revision Notes (6+ Years Interview)
Core Concepts
• Kafka → Distributed Event Streaming Platform.
• Topic → Logical channel to store messages.
• Partition → Splits topic for scalability.
• Broker → Kafka server.
• Producer → Publishes messages.
• Consumer → Reads messages.
• Offset → Unique message position inside partition.
________________________________________
Consumer Group
• Consumer Group → Multiple consumers working together.
• Same Group → One message consumed by one consumer.
• Different Groups → Same message can be consumed by all groups.
1 Partition = 1 Consumer in a Group
________________________________________
Replication
• Replication → Copies data across brokers.
• Leader → Handles Read/Write.
• Follower → Replicates Leader data.
• ISR (In-Sync Replicas) → Replicas synced with leader.
Replication provides Fault Tolerance.
________________________________________
Delivery Guarantees
At Most Once
• No duplicates
• Possible data loss
At Least Once
• No data loss
• Possible duplicates
Exactly Once (EOS)
• No duplicates
• No data loss
EOS = Idempotent Producer + Transactions
________________________________________
Producer Acknowledgement
• acks=0 → No confirmation.
• acks=1 → Leader confirmation.
• acks=all → All replicas confirmation (most reliable).
________________________________________
Idempotent Producer
enable.idempotence=true
Prevents duplicate messages.
________________________________________
Consumer Lag
Lag = Latest Offset - Consumer Offset
Example:
Latest Offset = 100
Consumer Offset = 80
Lag = 20
Higher lag = Slow Consumer.
________________________________________
Rebalancing
Occurs when:
• Consumer joins
• Consumer leaves
• Consumer crashes
Kafka redistributes partitions automatically.
________________________________________
Retention
• Messages remain in Kafka after consumption.
• Enables replay and recovery.
log.retention.hours=168
________________________________________
DLQ (Dead Letter Queue)
• Failed messages moved to separate topic.
orders-topic
↓
orders-dlq
Stores unprocessed messages.
