Interview Answer (1-Minute)
"The release process starts when developers commit code to Git. After code review and PR approval, Jenkins triggers a CI/CD pipeline that builds the application, runs JUnit tests, performs code quality checks using SonarQube, and creates an artifact. The artifact is deployed to Dev, QA, and UAT environments for testing. Once business approval is received, the release is deployed to Production using deployment strategies like Blue-Green or Rolling deployment. Finally, smoke testing and monitoring are performed to ensure the release is successful."
Difference Between CI and CD
•
CI (Continuous Integration): Build, test, and validate code automatically after every commit.
•
CD (Continuous Deployment/Delivery): Automatically deploy validated code to environments, including Production.
Smoke Testing
is a type of testing performed after a new build is deployed to verify that the application's critical functionalities are working and the build is stable enough for further testing.
It is also called Build Verification Testing (BVT).
Purpose
•
Verify the build is working.
•
Ensure critical features are not broken.
•
Decide whether to proceed with detailed testing.
Example
Suppose an e-commerce application is deployed.
Smoke test checks: Application launches successfully User can log in Home page loads Product search works Add-to-cart works Checkout page opens
It does not test every scenario in detail.
